<!--
  Sample Past Exam 3, with solutions.

  Transcribed from the instructor's solutions PDF of a past third midterm
  (Exam_3-MATH13-Spring2026-Solutions): its six questions and their
  handwritten solutions, with the four graphs of Question 2 redrawn in
  TikZ as outlines (no shading).  At the instructor's request the
  original Question 5, an ellipse from its foci and major axis (a conics
  question, not an Exam 3 topic this quarter), was replaced by a
  conceptual question on partial derivatives of the same 2 points: can
  f_x = x + 2y and f_y = 3x - y belong to one function?  (No, by the Mixed
  Derivative Theorem.)

  Laid out like Sample Past Exam 1: the points in each question's
  title and, for the parts, at the end of the part.

  At the instructor's request a seventh question, on tangent planes, was
  added (that topic is on this quarter's Exam 3 but was not on the past
  exam), and the points were redistributed to keep the total at 20:
  Questions 1 to 6 were worth 4, 4, 4, 3, 2 and 3 on the cover page
  (Question 6 was headed 2 points, one for each part), and are now worth
  3, 3, 4, 3, 2 and 2, with 3 for the new Question 7.

  Question 6(A) carries the restriction m not equal to 0, which the
  instructor wrote in by hand on the key; without it the solution's own
  line y = 0 would be one of the lines y = mx.

  So far only in MATH13-Instructor, included after Review Problems #3.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-sample-past-exam-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Sample Past Exam 3</title>

<introduction>
<p>
A sample past exam, with worked solutions. It was a 65-minute, closed-book
exam worth 20 points: no aids of their own (formula sheets, notes, phones,
tablets, smart watches or similar) were permitted, and students were asked
to write their work in a neat and organized format and to fully justify
every step. Try the exam under the same conditions before you look at the
solutions. The topics it covers, with links to the notes:
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li><p><xref ref="section-quadric-surfaces" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-limits-continuity" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-partial-derivatives" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-chain-rule" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-directional-derivatives" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-tangent-planes-differentials" text="title"/></p></li>
</ul>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ===================== Question 1 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe3-q1">
<title>3 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Determine whether
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{2x^2y}{x^4+y^2}
</md>
exists or not. If it does, find the limit; if it does not, fully justify
your answer.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Compare two paths into the origin. Along the <m>y</m>-axis, <m>x = 0</m>:
<md>
\lim_{y\to 0} \frac{0}{y^2} = \lim_{y\to 0} 0 = 0.
</md>
Along the parabola <m>y = x^2</m>:
<md>
\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{2x^2\cdot x^2}{x^4 + x^4}
= \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{2x^4}{2x^4} = 1.
</md>
The two paths give different limits, <m>0 \ne 1</m>, so
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{2x^2y}{x^4+y^2} \quad\text{does not exist.}
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 2 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe3-q2">
<title>3 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Which graph corresponds to <m>2x^2 - y^2 + z^2 = 0</m>?
</p>
<image width="92%">
<shortdescription>Four surfaces, each drawn around a horizontal y axis with the z axis up and the x axis toward the viewer. Graph A is a hyperboloid of two sheets: two bowls opening away from each other along the y axis, with a gap between them. Graph B is a hyperboloid of one sheet: a single tube along the y axis with a narrow waist at the origin. Graph C is a double cone: two cones along the y axis meeting at their tips at the origin. Graph D is an elliptic paraboloid: one bowl with its vertex at the origin, opening along the positive y axis.</shortdescription>
<latex-image><![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.62, line join=round]
  \tikzset{surf/.style={draw=blue!55!black, thick},
           hid/.style={draw=blue!55!black, dashed},
           ax/.style={-stealth, black!75}}
  % ---------------- Graph A: hyperboloid of two sheets ----------------
  \begin{scope}[shift={(0,0)}]
    \node[draw, fill=white, font=\small] at (-2.6,2.4) {Graph A};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (0,2.1) node[above] {$z$};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (-1.1,-1.1) node[below left] {$x$};
    \draw[surf] plot[domain=-1.25:1.25, samples=40] ({1+\x*\x*0.9},{\x});
    \draw[surf] (2.406,0) ellipse (0.32 and 1.25);
    \draw[surf] plot[domain=-1.25:1.25, samples=40] ({-1-\x*\x*0.9},{\x});
    \draw[surf] (-2.406,0) ellipse (0.32 and 1.25);
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \end{scope}
  % ---------------- Graph B: hyperboloid of one sheet ----------------
  \begin{scope}[shift={(8.2,0)}]
    \node[draw, fill=white, font=\small] at (-2.6,2.4) {Graph B};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (0,2.1) node[above] {$z$};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (-1.1,-1.1) node[below left] {$x$};
    \draw[surf] plot[domain=-2.2:2.2, samples=40] ({\x},{0.55*sqrt(1+\x*\x)});
    \draw[surf] plot[domain=-2.2:2.2, samples=40] ({\x},{-0.55*sqrt(1+\x*\x)});
    \draw[surf] (2.2,0) ellipse (0.3 and 1.329);
    \draw[surf] (-2.2,0) ellipse (0.3 and 1.329);
    \draw[hid] (0,0) ellipse (0.14 and 0.55);
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \end{scope}
  % ---------------- Graph C: double cone ----------------
  \begin{scope}[shift={(0,-5.6)}]
    \node[draw, fill=white, font=\small] at (-2.6,2.4) {Graph C};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (0,2.1) node[above] {$z$};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (-1.1,-1.1) node[below left] {$x$};
    \draw[surf] (2.3,1.15) -- (-2.3,-1.15);
    \draw[surf] (2.3,-1.15) -- (-2.3,1.15);
    \draw[surf] (2.3,0) ellipse (0.3 and 1.15);
    \draw[surf] (-2.3,0) ellipse (0.3 and 1.15);
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \end{scope}
  % ---------------- Graph D: elliptic paraboloid ----------------
  \begin{scope}[shift={(8.2,-5.6)}]
    \node[draw, fill=white, font=\small] at (-2.6,2.4) {Graph D};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (0,2.1) node[above] {$z$};
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (-1.1,-1.1) node[below left] {$x$};
    \draw[surf] plot[domain=-1.4:1.4, samples=40] ({\x*\x*1.2},{\x});
    \draw[surf] (2.352,0) ellipse (0.34 and 1.4);
    \draw[ax] (0,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
<em>Note:</em> Fully justify your response. Correct answers with incorrect
or unclear justifications will not earn points.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Look at the traces of <m>2x^2 - y^2 + z^2 = 0</m> in the three coordinate
planes:
<md>
<mrow>y = 0: \amp\quad 2x^2 + z^2 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = z = 0,</mrow>
<mrow>z = 0: \amp\quad y^2 = 2x^2 \;\Rightarrow\; y = \pm\sqrt2\,x,</mrow>
<mrow>x = 0: \amp\quad z^2 = y^2 \;\Rightarrow\; z = \pm y.</mrow>
</md>
The first trace is only the origin; each of the other two is a pair of
lines through the origin.
The traces in the planes <m>y = k</m> are the ellipses
<m>2x^2 + z^2 = k^2</m>, a single point for <m>k = 0</m> and growing with
<m>\lvert k\rvert</m>. A surface that meets the plane <m>y = 0</m> only at
the origin, is cut in pairs of lines through the origin by the other two
coordinate planes, and has elliptical cross-sections growing linearly
along the <m>y</m>-axis is a double cone with the <m>y</m>-axis as its
axis:
<md>
y^2 = 2x^2 + z^2.
</md>
This is <alert>Graph C</alert>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 3 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe3-q3">
<title>4 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{x^2 - y^2}{x^2 + y^2}</m>. Find all the directions
<m>\hat u</m> such that <m>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{(1,1)} = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
By the quotient rule,
<md>
<mrow>f_x \amp = \frac{2x(x^2+y^2) - 2x(x^2-y^2)}{(x^2+y^2)^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{4xy^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2},</mrow>
<mrow>f_y \amp = \frac{-2y(x^2+y^2) - 2y(x^2-y^2)}{(x^2+y^2)^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{-4x^2y}{(x^2+y^2)^2},</mrow>
</md>
so <m>f_x(1,1) = \frac44 = 1</m>, <m>f_y(1,1) = -\frac44 = -1</m>, and
<m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} = \langle 1, -1\rangle</m>. For a unit
vector <m>\hat u = \langle u_1, u_2\rangle</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{(1,1)}
\amp = \langle 1, -1\rangle\cdot\langle u_1, u_2\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = u_1 - u_2 = 0,</mrow>
</md>
so <m>u_1 = u_2</m>. Since <m>\hat u</m> is a unit vector, <m>u_1^2 + u_2^2 = 1</m>, so
<m>2u_1^2 = 1</m> and <m>u_1 = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt2}</m>. The two directions
are
<md>
<mrow>\hat u \amp = \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle
\qquad\text{and}</mrow>
<mrow>\hat u \amp = \left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle,</mrow>
</md>
the two unit vectors perpendicular to <m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 4 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe3-q4">
<title>3 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
The volume of a right circular cone is <m>V = \frac13\pi r^2 h</m>, where
<m>r</m> is the radius of the base and <m>h</m> is the height of the cone.
The radius is increasing at a rate of 3 inches per second and the height
is decreasing at a rate of 5 inches per second. At what rate is the volume
of the cone changing when the radius is 9 inches and the height is 9
inches? (Remember to include the units for your final answer.)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The data are <m>\dfrac{dr}{dt} = 3</m> and <m>\dfrac{dh}{dt} = -5</m>, at
the instant when <m>r = h = 9</m>. The volume depends on <m>t</m> through
<m>r</m> and <m>h</m>, so by the chain rule
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dV}{dt} \amp = \frac{\partial V}{\partial r}\frac{dr}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial V}{\partial h}\frac{dh}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(\frac{2\pi}{3}rh\right)(3)
+ \left(\frac13\pi r^2\right)(-5).</mrow>
</md>
At <m>(r,h) = (9,9)</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dV}{dt}\bigg|_{(9,9)}
\amp = \frac13\left[(6\pi)(9)(9) - (5\pi)(9)^2\right]</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{81\pi}{3} = 27\pi \ \ \frac{\text{in}^3}{\text{s}}.</mrow>
</md>
The volume is increasing at <m>27\pi</m> cubic inches per second.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 5 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe3-q5">
<title>2 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Is there a function <m>f(x,y)</m>, with continuous second partial
derivatives everywhere, such that
<md>
<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = x + 2y \qquad\text{and}</mrow>
<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = 3x - y\ ?</mrow>
</md>
Fully justify your answer.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>No.</alert> If there were such an <m>f</m>, its two mixed partial
derivatives would be
<md>
<mrow>f_{xy} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\bigl(f_x\bigr)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x + 2y) = 2,</mrow>
<mrow>f_{yx} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\bigl(f_y\bigr)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(3x - y) = 3 .</mrow>
</md>
But <m>f</m> and all its partial derivatives up to order two are
continuous, so by the Mixed Derivative Theorem,
<xref ref="thm-mixed-derivative"/>, <m>f_{xy} = f_{yx}</m> at every point.
Since <m>2 \ne 3</m>, no such function exists.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 6 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe3-q6">
<title>2 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Mark the following statements as <alert>True</alert> or
<alert>False</alert>. If a statement is true, prove it, and if a statement
is false, provide a counterexample.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="spe3-q6-a">
<statement>
<p>
Assume that we evaluate <m>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m>
along the lines <m>y = mx</m> (<m>m \ne 0</m>). If the result of
<m>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> is independent of
<m>m</m>, then the limit <m>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m>
exists at <m>(a,b)</m>. (1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>False.</alert> A counterexample at <m>(a,b) = (0,0)</m> is
<m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{x^2}{x^2 + y}</m>. Along a line <m>y = mx</m> with
<m>m \ne 0</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x^2}{x^2 + mx}
\amp = \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x^2}{x(x+m)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x}{x+m} = 0,</mrow>
</md>
the same for every <m>m \ne 0</m>. But along the line <m>y = 0</m>,
<md>
\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x^2}{x^2} = 1 \ne 0,
</md>
so <m>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{x^2}{x^2+y}</m> does not
exist.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe3-q6-b">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> exists along any path
that goes through <m>(a,b)</m>, then the function <m>f(x,y)</m> is
<alert>continuous</alert> at the point <m>(a,b)</m>. (1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>False.</alert> Take <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{x^2 - y^2}{x + y}</m> at
<m>(a,b) = (0,0)</m>. Wherever <m>f</m> is defined, <m>x + y \ne 0</m> and
<m>f(x,y) = x - y</m>, so along every path
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{x^2 - y^2}{x + y}
\amp = \lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} (x - y)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0 .</mrow>
</md>
However, <m>f</m> is not defined at <m>(0,0)</m>, so it is not continuous
there: continuity needs the limit to exist <em>and</em> to equal the value
of the function at the point.
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 7 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe3-q7">
<title>3 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Consider the ellipsoid <m>x^2 + 2y^2 + 3z^2 = 21</m> and the point
<m>P_0(1,-2,2)</m> on it.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="spe3-q7-a">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation of the tangent plane to the ellipsoid at <m>P_0</m>.
(2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First check that <m>P_0</m> is on the surface:
<m>1 + 2(4) + 3(4) = 21</m>. The ellipsoid is the level surface
<m>F(x,y,z) = 21</m> of <m>F(x,y,z) = x^2 + 2y^2 + 3z^2</m>, and the
gradient of <m>F</m> is normal to its level surfaces:
<md>
<mrow>\nabla F \amp = \langle 2x,\ 4y,\ 6z\rangle,</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla F\big|_{P_0} \amp = \langle 2,\ -8,\ 12\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
Any nonzero multiple is also normal, so take
<m>\vec n = \frac12\nabla F\big|_{P_0} = \langle 1, -4, 6\rangle</m>.
The tangent plane through <m>P_0</m> with normal <m>\vec n</m> is
<md>
<mrow>\amp (x - 1) - 4(y + 2) + 6(z - 2) = 0,</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \text{that is,}\quad x - 4y + 6z = 21 .</mrow>
</md>
Check: <m>P_0</m> satisfies it, since <m>1 + 8 + 12 = 21</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe3-q7-b">
<statement>
<p>
Find parametric equations of the line normal to the ellipsoid at
<m>P_0</m>. (1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The normal line passes through <m>P_0(1,-2,2)</m> in the direction of
<m>\vec n = \langle 1, -4, 6\rangle</m>:
<md>
<mrow>x = 1 + t, \qquad y = -2 - 4t,</mrow>
<mrow>z = 2 + 6t, \qquad t \in \mathbb{R}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
